<p class="sst-lead">A shape begins as a <em>boundary curve</em> &mdash; a Lipschitz immersion that traces an
object's outline in the plane.</p>

<p>\[ \bm{c}:\mathcal{I}\subset\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}:s\mapsto\big(c_1(s),\dots,c_d(s)\big)^{\top} \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Sampling the curve at landmarks \(s_1,\dots,s_n\) gives a <strong>discrete shape</strong>, a centered
  landmark matrix</p>
  <p>\[ X=(\bm{x}_1,\dots,\bm{x}_n)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 2}_{*},\qquad \bm{x}_i=\bm{c}(s_i). \]</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Two images of the <em>same</em> shape can differ by a rigid motion &mdash; rotation, reflection,
  translation &mdash; and by how we <em>index</em> the landmarks. The whole game is to measure shape
  <strong>up to</strong> those nuisances.</p>
</div>
